{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2635 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "603214954a25174053bd9f099e94f6c28aacb1559f7ffe9a94194c1a3474ef23" :edb/db-before-t 2634 :edb/db-after-t 2635 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535947 50 #inst "2026-10-15T10:35:37.544Z" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186064276 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547040 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186064333 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546965 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186068710 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547009 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186068726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547069 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186068785 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547031 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186068850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547039 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186068919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547044 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186068919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547046 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186068920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546981 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186068920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546984 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186068920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547038 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186068920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547049 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186068920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547050 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186068921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546978 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186068921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547030 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186068921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547073 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186071348 1078 #edb/ref 17592186071349 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186073752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546971 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186073753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546991 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186073788 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547071 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186074185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186074185 1071 "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $p - q$?\n\n${3p = 15; p + 2q = 9$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186074185 1071 "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $p-q?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3p=15 \\\\ p+2q=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186074185 1074 "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $p$ by dividing both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p & =15 \\\\ p & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $p=5$ into $p+2q=9,$ then we can find the value of $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+2q & =9 \\\\ (5)+2q & =9 \\\\ 2q & =4 \\\\ q & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=5,\\,q=2.$ So,\n\n$$\np-q=5-2=3.\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186078578 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547052 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186083179 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546999 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186083179 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547008 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186086178 1078 #edb/ref 17592186086180 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186086232 1075 #edb/ref 17592186086233 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186086232 1075 #edb/ref 17592186086234 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186086232 1075 #edb/ref 17592186086235 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186086232 1075 #edb/ref 17592186086236 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186086232 1075 #edb/ref 17592186086237 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186086232 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546351 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186086232 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546444 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186086232 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546463 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186086232 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546553 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186086232 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546631 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186086232 1078 #edb/ref 17592186546463 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186087494 1071 "What is the value of $x$ if $e^{x - 3}=2^5$?" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186087494 1071 "What is the value of $x$ if $e^{x - 3}=2^5?$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186087494 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm\n\n$$\n\\left(\\ln\\right)\n$$\n\nof both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x - 3} &= 2^{5} \\\\\n\\ln (e^{x - 3}) &= \\ln (2^{5})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\ln \\left(e^a \\right) = a\n$$\n\nto simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx-3 = \\ln \\left(2^5 \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$ and rewrite the right-hand side using the power law of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 3 &= \\ln (2^{5}) \\\\\nx &= \\ln (2^{5}) + 3 \\\\\nx &= 5\\ln (2) + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx=5\\ln (2) + 3\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186087494 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $\\left(\\ln\\right)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-3} & ={2}^{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\ln \\left( e^a \\right) = a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx-3 = \\ln \\left( 2^5 \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$ and rewrite the right-hand side using the power law of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5})+3 \\\\ x & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=5\\ln (2) + 3.$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186087495 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(\\ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{2 - 4x} &= 2 \\\\\n\\ln (e^{2 - 4x}) &= \\ln (2) \\\\\n2 - 4x &= \\ln (2) \\\\\n- 4x &= \\ln (2) - 2 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{2} - (\\ln (2))/(4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx \\approx 0.327\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186087495 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(\\ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2-4x} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2-4x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 2-4x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ -4x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx  \\approx 0.327\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186087496 1071 "Given that $e^{x+1} + 1 = 3$, what is the value of $x$?" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186087496 1071 "Given that $e^{x+1} + 1 = 3,$ what is the value of $x$?" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186087496 1074 "First, we isolate the exponential, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x + 1} + 1 &= 3 \\\\\ne^{x + 1} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm\n\n$$\n\\left(\\ln\\right)\n$$\n\nof both sides of the equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x + 1} &= 2 \\\\\n\\ln (e^{x + 1}) &= \\ln (2) \\\\\nx + 1 &= \\ln (2) \\\\\nx &= \\ln (2) - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx =\\ln\\left(2\\right) -1\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186087496 1074 "First, we isolate the exponential, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+1}+1 & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $\\left(\\ln\\right)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+1} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x+1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x =\\ln\\left(2\\right) -1.$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186087689 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546961 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186087689 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547056 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186087690 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547080 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186087691 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547019 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186087935 1050 480.0 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186088254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186088254 1071 "Find the solution to the equation $e^{y} = 7$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186088254 1071 "Find the solution to the equation ${e}^{y}=7$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186088254 1074 "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y} & =7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\ny≈1.946\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186088263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186088263 1071 "Given that $e^{2w + 1} + 2 = 6$, then $w =$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186088263 1071 "Given that ${e}^{2w+1}+2=6,$ then $w=$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186088263 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2w+1}+2=6 \\\\ {e}^{2w+1} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2w+1} & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2w+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ 2w+1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ 2w & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-1 \\\\ w & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $w=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-1).$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186088885 1064 "Suppose we are given an equation containing an exponential function, such as\n\n$$\ne^{t}= 12.\n$$\n\nWe can solve it in the same way that we usually solve exponential equations, by taking the log on both sides where the base of the logarithm is the base of the exponential.\n\nHere, the base of the exponential is $e,$ so we take the *natural* logarithm of both sides, since\n\n$$\n\\log_e{x} = \\ln{x}.\n$$\n\nTaking the natural logarithm of both sides, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & =12 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{t}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\ln \\left( e^a \\right) = a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nt = \\ln (12).\n$$\n\nThis is the solution! We often present the solution of an exponential equation as an exact solution in logarithmic form." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186088885 1064 "Suppose we are given an equation containing an exponential function, such as\n$e^{t}= 12$.\n\nWe can solve it in the same way that we usually solve exponential equations, by taking the log on both sides where the base of the logarithm is the base of the exponential.\n\nHere, the base of the exponential is $e$, so we take the *natural* logarithm of both sides, since\n\n$$\n\\log_e{x} = \\ln{x}\n$$\n\nTaking the natural logarithm of both sides, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{t} &= 12 \\\\\n\\ln (e^{t}) &= \\ln (12)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\ln \\left(e^a \\right) = a\n$$\n\nto simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nt = \\ln (12)\n$$\n\nThis is the solution! We often present the solution of an exponential equation as an exact solution in logarithmic form." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186090532 1078 #edb/ref 17592186090534 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186090790 1075 #edb/ref 17592186090791 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186090790 1075 #edb/ref 17592186090792 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186090790 1075 #edb/ref 17592186090793 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186090790 1075 #edb/ref 17592186090794 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186090790 1075 #edb/ref 17592186090795 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186090790 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546264 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186090790 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546534 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186090790 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546572 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186090790 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546788 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186090790 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546804 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186090790 1078 #edb/ref 17592186546264 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186092091 1071 "Find the **total** area of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=2\\cos(2\\theta)$ and $r=1$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7c023033b4d3e0fa6ea5a2d9866688e360b759fba6aa0a5dd6241e148d7b093d.png)" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186092091 1071 "Find the **total** area of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=2\\cos(2\\theta)$ and $r=1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7c023033b4d3e0fa6ea5a2d9866688e360b759fba6aa0a5dd6241e148d7b093d.png)" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186092091 1074 "Consider the rightmost pair of petals. First, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cos (2θ) &= 1 \\\\\n\\cos (2θ) &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $2\\theta \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]$:\n\n$$\n2\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/1d/1dc9ce9add3d7135f69c92590ebde003d4e5ee31446d6202222ac890e2f42a9c.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1$, and\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\cos(2\\theta)$.\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big(\\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad \\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{1} &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{- π/6}^{π/6}((2\\cos (2θ))^{2} - 1^{2})dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{- π/6}^{π/6}(4\\cos^{2} (2θ) - 1)dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{- π/6}^{π/6}(2(1 + \\cos (4θ)) - 1)dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{- π/6}^{π/6}(1 + 2\\cos (4θ))dθ \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{2}θ\\right|_{- π/6}^{π/6} + \\left.\\frac{1}{4}\\sin (4θ)\\right|_{- π/6}^{π/6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{π}{6} - (-\\frac{π}{6})) + \\frac{1}{4}(\\sin \\frac{2π}{3} - \\sin (-\\frac{2π}{3})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6} + \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} - (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= 4A_{1} = 4(\\frac{π}{6} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}) = \\frac{2π}{3} + \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186092091 1074 "Consider the rightmost pair of petals. First, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $2\\theta \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]$:\n\n$$\n2\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/1d/1dc9ce9add3d7135f69c92590ebde003d4e5ee31446d6202222ac890e2f42a9c.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\cos(2\\theta).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big( \\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}-{1}^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(4{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))+\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4{A}_{1}=4(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186092092 1071 "Find the shaded area that lies between the polar curves $r=\\sin\\theta$ and $r=2\\sin\\theta$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/23/2389273b09df3e9bc9262a2daed848fbd518707e683e5d06a8dd0217766036bb.png)" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186092092 1071 "Find the shaded area that lies between the polar curves $r=\\sin\\theta$ and $r=2\\sin\\theta,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/23/2389273b09df3e9bc9262a2daed848fbd518707e683e5d06a8dd0217766036bb.png)" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186092092 1074 "First, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sin θ &= \\sin θ \\\\\n\\sin θ &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for\n\n$$\n\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\pi\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\n\\theta = 0, \\pi\n$$\n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/4c/4ce437c0d6b1f196269c5c1e56e687a6dca21fd407834dce20bfa7ac76298002.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=\\sin\\theta$, and\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\sin\\theta$.\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big(\\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\theta_1=0, \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad \\theta_2=\\pi\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{π}((2\\sin θ)^{2} - (\\sin θ)^{2})dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{π}3\\sin^{2} θdθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4}\\int_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos 2θ)dθ \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{3}{4}(θ - \\frac{\\sin 2θ}{2})\\right|_{0}^{π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4}[(π - (\\sin (2π))/(2)) - (0 - \\frac{\\sin 0}{2})] \\\\\n&= \\frac{3π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186092092 1074 "First, we find the values of $\\theta$ for