00$ | $118$ | $124$ | $86$ |\n\nYou are given that the means are $\\overline{x}=24$ and $\\overline{y}=107.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho\\approx 0.54$\n\nII. There is a strong positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\n*Hint: $|\\rho| > 0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3 \\leq |\\rho| \\leq 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096518 1074 "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ of the data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\big\\{y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\rho(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)}{\\sigma_x \\cdot \\sigma_y} = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\gt 0.7$, the correlation is *strong*\n- if $0.3\\leq \\mid \\rho \\mid \\leq 0.7$, the correlation is *weak*\n- if $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\lt 0.3$, then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. To calculate $\\rho$, we first compute the sum in the numerator:\n$S_{xy}|= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))(y_{i} - y^(―)); = (26 - 24)(100 - 107) + (30 - 24)(118 - 107); = + (22 - 24)(124 - 107) + (18 - 24)(86 - 107); = (2)(-7) + (6)(11) + (-2)(17) + (-6)(-21); =-14 + 66 - 34 + 126; = 144$\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n$S_{xx}|= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2}; = (26 - 24)^{2} + (30 - 24)^{2} + (22 - 24)^{2} + (18 - 24)^{2}; = 4 + 36 + 4 + 36; = 80; S_{yy}|= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(y_{i} - y^(―))^{2}; = (100 - 107)^{2} + (118 - 107)^{2} + (124 - 107)^{2} + (86 - 107)^{2}; = 49 + 121 + 289 + 441; = 900$\nFinally, we use the formula:\n$ρ(x, y)|= \\frac{144}{\\sqrt{80} \\cdot \\sqrt{900}}; \\approx \\frac{144}{8.944272 \\cdot 30}; \\approx 0.54$,\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $0.3\\leq \\mid \\rho \\mid \\leq 0.7$ the correlation is weak. Also, since $\\rho$ is positive, the correlation is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186096518 1074 "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ of the data sets $x=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}$ and $y=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho(x,y) =  \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)}{\\sigma_x \\cdot \\sigma_y} = \n\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho| \\gt 0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\leq |\\rho| \\leq 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho| \\lt 0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. To calculate $\\rho,$ we first compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(26-24)(100-107)+(30-24)(118-107) \\\\  & +(22-24)(124-107)+(18-24)(86-107) \\\\  & =(2)(-7)+(6)(11)+(-2)(17)+(-6)(-21) \\\\  & =-14+66-34+126 \\\\  & =144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(26-24)^{2}+(30-24)^{2}+(22-24)^{2}+(18-24)^{2} \\\\  & =4+36+4+36 \\\\  & =80 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(100-107)^{2}+(118-107)^{2}+(124-107)^{2}+(86-107)^{2} \\\\  & =49+121+289+441 \\\\  & =900\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{144}{\\sqrt{80}\\cdot \\sqrt{900}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{144}{8.944\\,272\\cdot 30} \\\\  & ≈0.54,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $0.3\\leq |\\rho| \\leq 0.7$ the correlation is weak. Also, since $\\rho$ is positive, the correlation is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096522 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx = \\big\\{ 6, \\: 3, \\: -1, \\: 0 \\big\\},\\qquad\ny = \\big\\{ 5, \\: 7, \\: -2, \\: 6 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overline{x}=2,$ $\\overline{y}=4,$ $\\sigma_x \\approx 2.739,$ and $\\sigma_y \\approx 3.536.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096522 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n$x = \\big\\{6, \\: 3, \\: -1, \\: 0 \\big\\},\\qquad y = \\big\\{5, \\: 7, \\: -2, \\: 6 \\big\\}$\nYou're given that $\\overline{x}=2$, $\\overline{y}=4$, $\\sigma_x \\approx 2.739$, and $\\sigma_y \\approx 3.536$. Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186096522 1074 "The (population) linear correlation coefficient of the data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\big\\{y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\rho(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)}{\\sigma_x \\cdot \\sigma_y}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance, and $\\sigma_x$ and $\\sigma_y$ are the standard deviations of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the covariance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov(x, y) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))(y_{i} - y^(―)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[(6 - 2)(5 - 4) + (3 - 2)(7 - 4) + (-1 - 2)(-2 - 4) + (0 - 2)(6 - 4)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[(4)(1) + (1)(3) + (-3)(-6) + (-2)(2)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[4 + 3 + 18 - 4] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 21 \\\\\n&= 5.25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ(x, y) &= (Cov(x, y))/(σ_{x} \\cdot σ_{y}) \\\\\n&\\approx \\frac{5.25}{2.739 \\cdot 3.536} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.54\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186096522 1074 "The (population) linear correlation coefficient of the data sets $x=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}$ and $y=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho(x,y) =  \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)}{\\sigma_x \\cdot \\sigma_y}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance, and $\\sigma_x$ and $\\sigma_y$ are the standard deviations of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the covariance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(6-2)(5-4)+(3-2)(7-4)+(-1-2)(-2-4)+(0-2)(6-4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4)(1)+(1)(3)+(-3)(-6)+(-2)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[4+3+18-4] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =5.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{5.25}{2.739\\cdot 3.536} \\\\  & ≈0.54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096593 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx = \\big\\{ 13, \\: 10, \\: 9, \\: 12 \\big\\},\\qquad\ny = \\big\\{ -1, \\: 3, \\: 2, \\: 4 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nYou are given that $\\overline{x}=11$ and $\\overline{y}=2.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096593 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n$x = \\big\\{13, \\: 10, \\: 9, \\: 12 \\big\\},\\qquad y = \\big\\{-1, \\: 3, \\: 2, \\: 4 \\big\\}$\nYou are given that $\\overline{x}=11$ and $\\overline{y}=2$. Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186096593 1074 "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ of the data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\big\\{y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\rho(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)}{\\sigma_x \\cdot \\sigma_y} = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{xy} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))(y_{i} - y^(―)) \\\\\n&= (13 - 11)(-1 - 2) + (10 - 11)(3 - 2) \\\\\n&= + (9 - 11)(2 - 2) + (12 - 11)(4 - 2) \\\\\n&= (2)(-3) + (-1)(1) + (-2)(0) + (1)(2) \\\\\n&=-6 - 1 + 0 + 2 \\\\\n&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{xx} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= (13 - 11)^{2} + (10 - 11)^{2} + (9 - 11)^{2} + (12 - 11)^{2} \\\\\n&= 4 + 1 + 4 + 1 \\\\\n&= 10 \\\\\nS_{yy} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(y_{i} - y^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= (-1 - 2)^{2} + (3 - 2)^{2} + (2 - 2)^{2} + (4 - 2)^{2} \\\\\n&= 9 + 1 + 0 + 4 \\\\\n&= 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ(x, y) &= ((-5))/(\\sqrt{10} \\cdot \\sqrt{14}) \\\\\n&\\approx - \\frac{5}{3.162278 \\cdot 3.741657} \\\\\n&\\approx - 0.42\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186096593 1074 "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ of the data sets $x=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}$ and $y=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho(x,y) =  \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)}{\\sigma_x \\cdot \\sigma_y} = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(13-11)(-1-2)+(10-11)(3-2) \\\\  & +(9-11)(2-2)+(12-11)(4-2) \\\\  & =(2)(-3)+(-1)(1)+(-2)(0)+(1)(2) \\\\  & =-6-1+0+2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(13-11)^{2}+(10-11)^{2}+(9-11)^{2}+(12-11)^{2} \\\\  & =4+1+4+1 \\\\  & =10 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(-1-2)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(4-2)^{2} \\\\  & =9+1+0+4 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-5)}{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & ≈-\\frac{5}{3.162\\,278\\cdot 3.741\\,657} \\\\  & ≈-0.42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096614 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547067 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096615 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547011 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096616 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547029 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096658 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547051 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096659 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547010 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096659 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547021 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546986 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546990 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547003 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547041 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547043 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546968 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546973 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546979 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546994 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546960 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546993 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547035 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547068 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547078 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096858 1050 780.0 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096927 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096927 1071 "Consider the data sets $x$ and $y$ such that $Cov(x, y) =-6$ and their standard deviations are $σ_{x} = 2$ and $σ_{y} = 5$. Compute the corresponding linear correlation coefficient.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186096927 1071 "Consider the data sets $x$ and $y$ such that $\\text{Cov}(x,y)=-6$ and their standard deviations are ${\\sigma }_{x}=2$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=5.$ Compute the corresponding linear correlation coefficient." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096927 1074 "The (population) linear correlation coefficient for data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our numbers, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-6)}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =-0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186097862 1064 "The **linear correlation coefficient**, also called the **Pearson correlation coefficient**, measures the strength of the linear relationship between two variables. It is often denoted by $\\rho$ for a population and by $r$ for a sample. In this lesson, we'll concern ourselves with population data only. \n\nThe linear correlation coefficient satisfies the following constraint:\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1\n$$\n\nWe interpret the endpoints of this interval as follows:\n\n- $\\rho = 1$ means that there is a perfect *positive* correlation between the data points.\n\n- $\\rho = -1$ means that there is a perfect *negative* correlation between the data points.\n\nMoreover, $\\rho = 0$ means there is no *linear* correlation between the data points. \n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- If $|\\rho| \\gt 0.7,$ the correlation is **strong,** and the data points closely follow a straight line. \n\n![](images/3f/3fd8b4e85bd76bbf78fdb2b135c49b3fd5e37fd9651dcae29d945d75aa45b905.png)\n\n- If $0.3\\leq |\\rho| \\leq 0.7,$ the correlation is **weak.** The data points follow a straight line, but many are far from the line.\n\n![](images/c1/c132ec90770bb84ad6c7dcc4b24773a923139c616f523ba0a23c1506c40c508c.png)\n\n- If $|\\rho| \\lt 0.3,$ then the correlation is **negligible.** There is no (linear) correlation.\n\n![](images/9a/9afa80bfadc40b8b832b17dcea10d66d5a08955909d5bea66c7c010558b5ea19.png)" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186097862 1064 "The **linear correlation coefficient,** denoted $\\rho$ (the Greek letter \"rho\"), is a number that gives the strength of the *linear* dependence between two sets of paired observations $(x,y)$.\n\nThe linear correlation coefficient satisfies the following constraint:\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1\n$$\n\nWe interpret the endpoints of this interval as follows:\n\n- $\\rho = 1$ means that there is a perfect *positive* correlation between the data points.\n- $\\rho = -1$ means that there is a perfect *negative* correlation between the data points.\n\nMoreover, $\\rho = 0$ means there is no *linear* correlation between the data points.\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- If $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\gt 0.7$, the correlation is **strong,** and the data points closely follow a straight line.![](images/3f/3fd8b4e85bd76bbf78fdb2b135c49b3fd5e37fd9651dcae29d945d75aa45b905.png)\n\n- If $0.3\\leq \\mid \\rho \\mid \\leq 0.7$, the correlation is **weak.** The data points follow a straight line, but many are far from the line.![](images/c1/c132ec90770bb84ad6c7dcc4b24773a923139c616f523ba0a23c1506c40c508c.png)\n\n- If $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\lt 0.3$, then the correlation is **negligible.** There is no (linear) correlation.![](images/9a/9afa80bfadc40b8b832b17dcea10d66d5a08955909d5bea66c7c010558b5ea19.png)" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186097891 1064 "Suppose we have data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\big\\{y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nof the same size.\n\nLet's now introduce the following notation:\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2, \\qquad S_{yy} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i-\\overline{y})^2, \\qquad S_{xy} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y})\n$$\n\nNotice that $S_{xx}, S_{yy}$, and $S_{xy}$ are the sums of square deviations found in the formulas for the variance of $x$, the variance of $y$, and covariance of $x$ and $y$, respectively.\n\nUsing the previous definition, we can show that the correlation coefficient can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\\,\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186097891 1064 "Suppose we have data sets $x=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}$ and $y=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}$ of the same size.\n\nLet's now introduce the following notation:\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2, \n\\qquad S_{yy} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i-\\overline{y})^2,\n\\qquad S_{xy} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y})\n$$\n\nNotice that $S_{xx}, S_{yy},$ and $S_{xy}$ are the sums of square deviations found in the formulas for the variance of $x,$ the variance of $y,$ and covariance of $x$ and $y,$ respectively. \n\nUsing the previous definition, we can show that the correlation coefficient can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\\,.\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186097902 1064 "To calculate $\\rho$, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ(x, y) &= (Cov(x, y))/(σ_{x} \\cdot σ_{y})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance between $x$ and $y$, and\n- $\\sigma_x$ and $\\sigma_y$ are the standard deviations of $x$ and $y$, respectively.\n\nFor example, consider data sets $x$ and $y$ such that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)=-9, \\qquad \\sigma_x=6, \\qquad \\sigma_y=2\n$$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ(x, y) &= ((-9))/((6) \\cdot (2)) \\\\\n&= ((-9))/(12) \\\\\n&=-0.75\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\gt 0.7$, the correlation is strong. Also, since $\\rho$ is negative, the correlation is negative.\n\nTherefore, there is a strong negative correlation between $x$ and $y$." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186097902 1064 "To calculate $\\rho,$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance between $x$ and $y,$ and\n\n- $\\sigma_x$ and $\\sigma_y$ are the standard deviations of $x$ and $y,$ respectively.\n\nFor example, consider data sets $x$ and $y$ such that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)=-9, \\qquad \\sigma_x=6, \\qquad \\sigma_y=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-9)}{(6)\\cdot (2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-9)}{12} \\\\  & =-0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|\\rho| \\gt 0.7,$ the correlation is strong. Also, since $\\rho$ is negative, the correlation is negative.\n\nTherefore, there is a strong negative correlation between $x$ and $y.$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186097903 1064 "Let's derive the following result:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of the correlation coefficient $\\rho$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ &= (Cov(x, y))/(σ_{x} \\cdot σ_{y})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = \\dfrac1n \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y}) = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{n}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ_{x} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{S_{xx}}{n}} \\\\\nσ_{y} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(y_{i} - y^(―))^{2}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{S_{yy}}{n}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our definition for $\\rho$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ &= (Cov(x, y))/(σ_{x} \\cdot σ_{y}) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{S_{xy}}{n}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{S_{xx}}{n}} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{S_{yy}}{n}}) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{S_{xy}}{n}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}{n^{2}}}) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{S_{xy}}{n}))/((\\frac{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}{n})) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{S_{xy}}{n}))/((\\frac{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}{n})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186097903 1064 "Let's derive the following result:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of the correlation coefficient $\\rho\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = \\dfrac1n \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y}) = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{n}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{S}_{xx}}{n}} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{S}_{yy}}{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our definition for $\\rho,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n})}{\\sqrt{\\frac{{S}_{xx}}{n}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{S}_{yy}}{n}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n})}{\\sqrt{\\frac{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}{{n}^{2}}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}{n})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\cancel{n}})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}{\\cancel{n}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186105850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547026 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186105903 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547048 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186105903 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547053 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186105905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547012 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186105905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547025 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186105906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547072 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186105906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547082 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186105954 1050 720.0 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186106917 1061 463833 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186106917 1061 655395 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186106918 1061 463834 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186106918 1061 655396 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186106919 1061 463837 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186106919 1061 655399 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186106920 1061 463840 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186106920 1061 655402 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186106921 1061 463843 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186106921 1061 655405 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186106922 1061 463846 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186106922 1061 655408 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186107027 1064 "Consider $\\triangle ABC$ with sides of lengths $a,b,$ and $c,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d30e12e2252b27a651bb02b718cf2214c7ddcf4f0e768c5bd952c11b2e4ecde.png)\n\nLet's now add the height of length $h$ perpendicular to $\\overline{BC},$ as shown.\n\n![](images/96/96817ab2168b9ec37777166a26001ee8bc7e7709f80fdf234ac6d70cdfbb5ddc.png)\n\nUsing the usual formula for the area $\\mathcal A$ of a triangle in terms of its base and height, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12 ah.\n$$\n\nUsing elementary trigonometry, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{h}{b} = \\sin C\n$$\n\nwhich means that \n\n$$\nh = b\\sin C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting this into our expression for $\\mathcal A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12ab\\sin C\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar approach to prove the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}B\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186107027 1064 "Consider $\\triangle ABC$ with sides of lengths $a,b,$ and $c,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d30e12e2252b27a651bb02b718cf2214c7ddcf4f0e768c5bd952c11b2e4ecde.png)\n\nLet's now add the height of length $h$ perpendicular to $\\overline{BC},$ as shown.\n\n![](images/96/96817ab2168b9ec37777166a26001ee8bc7e7709f80fdf234ac6d70cdfbb5ddc.png)\n\nUsing the usual formula for the area $\\mathcal A$ of a triangle in terms of its base and height, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12 ah.\n$$\n\nUsing elementary trigonometry, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{h}{b} = \\sin C\n$$\n\nwhich means that \n\n$$\nh = b\\sin C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting this into our expression for $\\mathcal A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12ab\\sin C\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar approach to prove the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}⁡B\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186107063 1064 "The formula for the area of a general triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}{\\color{red}{a}}{\\color{blue}{b}}\\sin{C},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{red}a$ and $\\color{blue}b$ are two sides of the triangle and $C$ is the measure of the angle between those sides (called the **included angle**).\n\nWe'll prove this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nTo demonstrate how this works, suppose that we want to calculate the area of the non-right-angled triangle below:\n\n![](images/4b/4b0fb75f9b2eaf3d0237d8cfe2e31bc27b02425b6cffa8d15f27d6de03f60838.png)\n\nLet's call the lengths of the sides $BC = \\color{red}{a}$ and $AC ={\\color{blue}{b}}.$ For the triangle above we have\n${\\color{red}a=10},$ ${\\color{blue}b=12},$ and  $m\\angle C=45^{\\circ}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of our triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 12\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =60\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =30\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186107063 1064 "The formula for the area of a general triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}{\\color{red}{a}}{\\color{blue}{b}}\\sin{C},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{red}a$ and $\\color{blue}b$ are two sides of the triangle and $C$ is the measure of the angle between those sides (called the **included angle**).\n\nWe'll prove this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nTo demonstrate how this works, suppose that we want to calculate the area of the non-right-angled triangle below:\n\n![](images/4b/4b0fb75f9b2eaf3d0237d8cfe2e31bc27b02425b6cffa8d15f27d6de03f60838.png)\n\nLet's call the lengths of the sides $BC = \\color{red}{a}$ and $AC ={\\color{blue}{b}}.$ For the triangle above we have\n${\\color{red}a=10},$ ${\\color{blue}b=12},$ and  $m\\angle C=45^{\\circ}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of our triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 12\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =60\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =30\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186110616 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547022 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186113713 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113714 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186113713 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113715 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186113713 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113716 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186113713 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113717 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186113713 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113718 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186113713 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546299 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186113713 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546496 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186113713 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546539 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186113713 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546549 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186113713 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546709 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186113713 1078 #edb/ref 17592186546496 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186114877 1078 #edb/ref 17592186114882 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547014 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547058 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115214 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546959 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115215 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546998 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115215 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547065 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115268 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546992 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115270 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547005 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115270 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547061 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546963 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546976 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115317 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547059 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115317 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547076 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547018 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547077 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546988 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115793 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113695 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186115796 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115796 1071 "A $9800mL$-capacity tank initially holds $7000mL$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $196g$ of salt. Fresh water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $105mL/s$, while the completely mixed brine flows out at a rate of $70mL/s$. Find the amount of salt in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186115796 1071 "A $9\\,800\\,\\text{mL}$-capacity tank initially holds $7\\,000\\,\\text{mL}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $196\\,\\text{g}$ of salt. Fresh water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $105\\,\\text{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed brine flows out at a rate of $70\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ Find the amount of salt in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115796 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $105\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (105\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (105\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $7\\,000\\,\\text{mL}$ of brine; fresh water enters at $105\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed brine leaves at $70\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the tank after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=7\\,000+105t-70t=35(200+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the concentration of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{35(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $70\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(70\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{35(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(70\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{35(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{200+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{200+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{200+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{200+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{200+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{200+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|200+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(200+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $196\\,\\text{g}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=196,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =196 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(200+0)^{2}} & =196 \\\\ A & =196(200)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{196(200)^{2}}{(200+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =196{(\\frac{200}{200+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $9\\,800\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=9\\,800$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =9\\,800 \\\\ 35(200+{t}_{o}) & =9\\,800 \\\\ 200+{t}_{o} & =280 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =80\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(80) & =196{(\\frac{200}{200+80})}^{2} \\\\  & =196{(\\frac{5}{7})}^{2} \\\\  & =100\\,\\text{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115990 1071 "Given that the random variable $X∼B(100, 0.4)$, use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X = 50)$. Give your final answer to three decimal places\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.04$ | $0.05$ | $0.06$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1.9$ | $0.9738$ | $0.9744$ | $0.9750$ |\n| $2.0$ | $0.9793$ | $0.9798$ | $0.9803$ |\n| $2.1$ | $0.9838$ | $0.9842$ | $0.9846$ |\n| $2.2$ | $0.9875$ | $0.9878$ | $0.9881$ |\n| $2.3$ | $0.9904$ | $0.9906$ | $0.9909$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186115990 1071 "Given that the random variable $X∼B(100,0.4),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X=50).$ Give your final answer to three decimal places\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.9738\\,$ | $\\,0.9744\\,$ | $\\,0.9750\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9793\\,$ | $\\,0.9798\\,$ | $\\,0.9803\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9838\\,$ | $\\,0.9842\\,$ | $\\,0.9846\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9875\\,$ | $\\,0.9878\\,$ | $\\,0.9881\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9904\\,$ | $\\,0.9906\\,$ | $\\,0.9909\\,$ |" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186115990 1074 "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=100$ and $p=0.4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100\\cdot (1-0.4) \\\\  & =60 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(100)(0.4)=40\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(100)(0.4)(1-0.4)=24.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(40,24).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=50).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=50)≈P(49.5\\le Y\\le 50.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(49.5\\le Y\\le 50.5) & =P(\\frac{49.5-40}{\\sqrt{24}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{50.5-40}{\\sqrt{24}}) \\\\  & ≈P(1.94\\le Z\\le 2.14) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.14)-P(Z<1.94) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.14)-P(Z\\le 1.94) \\\\  & =Φ(2.14)-Φ(1.94) \\\\  & =0.9838-0.9738 \\\\  & =0.010\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=50)≈0.010.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0102.$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186124533 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547037 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186124534 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547083 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186124662 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546967 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186159842 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159847 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186161706 1071 "The variances of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ are $25$ and $36$, respectively. Given that $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 18$, find the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y$." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186161706 1071 "The variances of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ are $25$ and $36,$ respectively. Given that $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 18,$ find the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186161706 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] }{\\textrm {SD}[X] \\cdot \\textrm {SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\textrm {SD}[X]$ and $\\textrm {SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{25}=5, \\\\ \\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[Y]}=\\sqrt{36}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our data in the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[X]} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{5\\cdot 6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186161706 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]}{\\textrm {SD}[X] \\cdot \\textrm {SD}[Y]}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]$ is the covariance, and\n- $\\textrm {SD}[X]$ and $\\textrm {SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSD[X] &= \\sqrt{Var[X]} = \\sqrt{25} = 5, \\\\\nSD[X] &= \\sqrt{Var[Y]} = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our data in the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ &= \\frac{Cov[X, Y]}{SD[X] \\cdot SD[X]} \\\\\n&= \\frac{18}{5 \\cdot 6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186161707 1071 "| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ | $0.6$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\textrm {E}[X]=0.4\\qquad\n\\textrm {E}[Y]=0.6, \\qquad\n\\textrm {SD}[X] \\approx 0.4899, \\qquad\n\\textrm {SD}[Y] \\approx 0.4899,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186161707 1071 "| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ | $0.6$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that\n$\\textrm {E}[X]=0.4\\qquad \\textrm {E}[Y]=0.6, \\qquad \\textrm {SD}[X] \\approx 0.4899, \\qquad \\textrm {SD}[Y] \\approx 0.4899$,\nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y$. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186161707 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] }{\\textrm {SD}[X] \\cdot \\textrm {SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\textrm {SD}[X]$ and $\\textrm {SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}xy\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0.1+0\\cdot 1\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & +1\\cdot 0\\cdot 0.3+1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =0.1-(0.4)(0.6) \\\\  & =-0.14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{-0.14}{0.4899\\cdot 0.4899} \\\\  & ≈-0.58,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186161707 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]}{\\textrm {SD}[X] \\cdot \\textrm {SD}[Y]}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]$ is the covariance, and\n- $\\textrm {SD}[X]$ and $\\textrm {SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y$.\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[XY] &= ∑_(x ∈ S_{X})∑_(y ∈ S_{Y})xyf(x, y) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 0 \\cdot 0.1 + 0 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 0.5 \\\\\n&= + 1 \\cdot 0 \\cdot 0.3 + 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 0.1 \\\\\n&= 0.1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[X, Y] &= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= 0.1 - (0.4)(0.6) \\\\\n&=-0.14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ &= \\frac{Cov[X, Y]}{SD[X] \\cdot SD[Y]} \\\\\n&\\approx \\frac{-0.14}{0.4899 \\cdot 0.4899} \\\\\n&\\approx - 0.58\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186161708 1071 "![](images/9c/9cf18d64c3f283c730e1d32ee81e3f193e615bb7b0b936dac009c46328826b3d.svg)\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\textrm {E}[X]= \\dfrac{3}{4},\\qquad\n\\textrm {E}[Y]= \\dfrac{5}{12}, \\qquad\n\\textrm {SD}[X]\\approx 0.1936, \\qquad\n\\textrm {SD}[Y]\\approx 0.2444,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186161708 1071 "${2(x + y),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le y \\le x \\le 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that\n$\\textrm {E}[X]= \\dfrac{3}{4},\\qquad \\textrm {E}[Y]= \\dfrac{5}{12}, \\qquad \\textrm {SD}[X]\\approx 0.1936, \\qquad \\textrm {SD}[Y]\\approx 0.2444$,\nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y$. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186161708 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] }{\\textrm {SD}[X] \\cdot \\textrm {SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\textrm {SD}[X]$ and $\\textrm {SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}xy\\cdot 2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}(2{x}^{2}y+2x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}x{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{5}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{(\\frac{1}{48})}{(0.1936)\\cdot (0.2444)} \\\\  & ≈0.44,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186161708 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]}{\\textrm {SD}[X] \\cdot \\textrm {SD}[Y]}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]$ is the covariance, and\n- $\\textrm {SD}[X]$ and $\\textrm {SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y$.\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[XY] &= ∬_{R^{2}}xyf(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[\\int_{0}^{x}xy \\cdot 2(x + y)dy]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[\\int_{0}^{x}(2x^{2}y + 2xy^{2})dy]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[x^{2}y^{2} + \\frac{2}{3}xy^{3}]_{y = 0}^{y = x}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(x^{4} + \\frac{2}{3}x^{4})dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{5}{3}x^{4}dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{3}x^{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[X, Y] &= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} - (\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{5}{12}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{48}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ &= \\frac{Cov[X, Y]}{SD[X] \\cdot SD[Y]} \\\\\n&\\approx ((\\frac{1}{48}))/((0.1936) \\cdot (0.2444)) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.44\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186162140 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546966 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186162140 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546969 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186162140 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546972 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186162140 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547017 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186162141 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547020 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186162141 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547024 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186162142 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546977 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186162142 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546989 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186162142 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547013 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186162142 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547028 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186162142 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547063 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186162188 1050 840.0 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186162440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186162440 1071 "Given that $Cov[X, Y] = 5$, $SD[X] = 2$, and $SD[Y] = 4$, find the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186162440 1071 "Given that $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=5,$ $\\text{SD}[X]=2,$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]=4$, find the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186162440 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nSubstituting our data in the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186163294 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two random variables with covariance $\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]$ and standard deviations $\\textrm {SD}[X]$ and $\\textrm {SD}[Y]$.\n\nThe **correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y$,** also known as the **Pearson correlation coefficient**, is denoted $\\rho$ (the Greek letter \"rho\") and is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]}{\\textrm {SD}[X]\\cdot \\textrm {SD}[Y]}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The correlation coefficient satisfies\n$-1\\leq \\rho \\leq 1$.\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then\n$\\rho = 0$.\nThe reverse implication does not always hold. In other words, $\\rho=0$ does *not* imply that $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n- Unlike the covariance, $\\rho$ is a *dimensionless* quantity. This means we'll get the same answer regardless of the units used to measure $X$ and $Y$.\n- Also unlike the covariance, $\\rho$ tells the strength of the correlation between $X$ and $Y$. We typically have the following rule of thumb:If $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\gt 0.7$, the correlation is **strong,** and the data generated by our two random variables closely follow a straight line.![](images/3f/3fd8b4e85bd76bbf78fdb2b135c49b3fd5e37fd9651dcae29d945d75aa45b905.png)If $0.3\\leq \\mid \\rho \\mid \\leq 0.7$, the correlation is **weak.** The data generated by our random variables follow a straight line, but many are far from the line.![](images/c1/c132ec90770bb84ad6c7dcc4b24773a923139c616f523ba0a23c1506c40c508c.png)If $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\lt 0.3$, then the correlation is **negligible.** No (linear) correlation exists between the data points generated by the random variables.![](images/9a/9afa80bfadc40b8b832b17dcea10d66d5a08955909d5bea66c7c010558b5ea19.png)\n\nLet's get some practice computing the correlation coefficient." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186163294 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two random variables with covariance $\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]$ and standard deviations $\\textrm {SD}[X]$ and $\\textrm {SD}[Y].$ \n\nThe **correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y,$** also known as the **Pearson correlation coefficient**, is denoted $\\rho$ (the Greek letter \"rho\") and is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] }{\\textrm {SD}[X]\\cdot \\textrm {SD}[Y]}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The correlation coefficient satisfies\n\n$$\n-1\\leq \\rho \\leq 1.\n$$\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then \n\n$$\n\\rho = 0.\n$$\n\nThe reverse implication does not always hold. In other words, $\\rho=0$ does *not* imply that $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\n- Unlike the covariance, $\\rho$ is a *dimensionless* quantity. This means we'll get the same answer regardless of the units used to measure $X$ and $Y.$\n\n- Also unlike the covariance, $\\rho$ tells the strength of the correlation between $X$ and $Y.$ We typically have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- If $|\\rho| \\gt 0.7,$ the correlation is **strong,** and the data generated by our two random variables closely follow a straight line. \n\n![](images/3f/3fd8b4e85bd76bbf78fdb2b135c49b3fd5e37fd9651dcae29d945d75aa45b905.png)\n\n- If $0.3\\leq |\\rho| \\leq 0.7,$ the correlation is **weak.** The data generated by our random variables follow a straight line, but many are far from the line.\n\n![](images/c1/c132ec90770bb84ad6c7dcc4b24773a923139c616f523ba0a23c1506c40c508c.png)\n\n- If $|\\rho| \\lt 0.3,$ then the correlation is **negligible.** No (linear) correlation exists between the data points generated by the random variables.\n\n![](images/9a/9afa80bfadc40b8b832b17dcea10d66d5a08955909d5bea66c7c010558b5ea19.png)\n\nLet's get some practice computing the correlation coefficient." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186164892 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546814 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186164892 1078 #edb/ref 17592186546814 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186164899 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546540 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186164899 1078 #edb/ref 17592186546540 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186166952 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546996 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [1759218616695