ame{sin}⁡(6x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-6x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+6x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{18}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-1-0)-\\frac{1}{18}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186167281 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186167281 1071 "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186167281 1071 "Evaluate the following integral.$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\sin (3x)\\cos (6x)dx =$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186167281 1074 "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+6x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-6x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}(0-1)+\\frac{1}{6}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186168040 1064 "When an integrand contains a product of trigonometric functions, we can often rewrite that product as a sum using the sum and difference formulas. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta) + \\cos(\\alpha+\\beta) = 2 \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta,$ we obtain the following **product-to-sum identity**:\n\n$$\n\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta)+\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\cos(5x)\\cos(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(5x)\\operatorname{cos}(3x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(5x-3x)+\\operatorname{cos}(5x+3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}(8x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}(5x)\\operatorname{cos}(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}(2x)+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}(8x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186168040 1064 "When an integrand contains a product of trigonometric functions, we can often rewrite that product as a sum using the sum and difference formulas. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta) + \\cos(\\alpha+\\beta) = 2 \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta,$ we obtain the following **product-to-sum identity**:\n\n$$\n\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta)+\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\cos(5x)\\cos(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x-3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186168041 1064 "Similar to the above, when an integrand contains a product of sines, we can rewrite that product as a sum of cosines using the sum and difference formulas for cosine. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta) - \\cos(\\alpha+\\beta) = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta,$ we obtain the following *product-to-sum identity*:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta)-\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(7x)\\sin(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(7x)\\operatorname{sin}(4x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(7x-4x)-\\operatorname{cos}(7x+4x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}(11x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}(7x