f 17592186546265 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186173746 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546357 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186173746 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546459 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186173746 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546769 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186173746 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546789 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186173746 1078 #edb/ref 17592186546769 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186176248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186176248 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $z = x^{2} + y^{2}$ that is subject to the constraint $x^{2} + y^{2} \\le 1$." 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186176248 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ that is subject to the constraint ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186176248 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f0faa92e0f89c86248abffad7f61cac5d94a3848a7f7be4464c4b28d3915aa32.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+4{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=8r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[{5}^{3/2}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (5\\sqrt{5}-1)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186197534 1078 #edb/ref 17592186197535 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186198247 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547006 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186198247 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547033 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186198248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546982 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186198248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547054 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186198944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186198944 1071 "![](images/72/720713e7e1dbfbc9ce3ebb486e8744327c633745d00df7dc862d794eefe57bbc.png)\n\nWhat is the least distance between the curve $y = \\sqrt{x}$ and the point $(4, 0)$?" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186198944 1071 "![](images/72/720713e7e1dbfbc9ce3ebb486e8744327c633745d00df7dc862d794eefe57bbc.png)\n\nWhat is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and the point $(4,0)?$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186198944 1074 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(4,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-7x+16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-7 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =7 \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{7}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{7}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{7}{2})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{7}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-7(\\frac{7}{2})+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{15}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186220357 1078 #edb/ref 17592186220361 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186220363 1075 #edb/ref 17592186220364 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186220363 1075 #edb/ref 17592186220365 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186220363 1075 #edb/ref 17592186220366 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186220363 1075 #edb/ref 17592186220367 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186220363 1075 #edb/ref 17592186220368 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186220363 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546274 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186220363 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546346 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186220363 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546374 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186220363 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546525 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186220363 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546647 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186220363 1078 #edb/ref 17592186546647 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186221405 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546958 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186221405 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547042 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186221406 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546987 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186221406 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547001 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186221952 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186221952 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186221952 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = x(t) - y(t); y^{′}(t) = x(t) - y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $λ = 0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v = [1, 1]^{T}$. The vector $w = [1, 0]^{T}$ is such that $(A - λI)w = v$. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186221952 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/30/309cbea2fa350c44c7a9225ec046b827113f9ffaa1dea6d746ab097e66cf945a.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+2 \\\\ y=2t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/d6/d6ec97d8fc3afffe41dca1dfd9d1918de586220408584183ef2eaad52e502485.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2t-2 \\\\ y=-2t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/09/0990ae7edeef543034fad206797305c41378b8243f809f5914f67c1e3b3955aa.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/af/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png)" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186221953 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186221953 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186221953 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) = 2x(t) + 2y(t)$\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186221953 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y=0 \\\\ 2x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186222488 1064 "Suppose a $2 \\times 2$ system has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2\\neq 0$ with eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2.$\n\nThen, the eigenvector direction for $\\lambda_1=0$ gives a **line of equilibria**:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_E = c_1 \\mathbf{v}_1, \\qquad c_1 \\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nThe other eigenvalue $\\lambda_2$ determines what happens off the equilibrium line:\n\n- If $\\lambda_2>0,$ trajectories move *away* from the equilibrium line (*unstable* direction).\n\n- If $\\lambda_2<0,$ trajectories move *toward* the equilibrium line (*stable* direction).\n\nA typical solution has the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1\\mathbf{v}_1 + c_2 e^{\\lambda_2 t}\\mathbf{v}_2,\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nSo trajectories are *parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_2,$* shifted by the equilibrium vector $c_1\\mathbf{v}_1.$\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following example\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere we know the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2=3,$ with corresponding eigenvectors ![](images/47/47c0d53d1eed7289cee7d61cfaf1e8a62bccc084f445b5ca217c9b3bec78b0d8.svg) and ![](images/99/998a77eb9b491e2d1e733a0dfe1a65cc1d39759650e36c671ca1c2aa1064e29a.svg) respectively. \n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system. \n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](images/e7/e71f95804ab65c6d60a907f33967a744b13e2c977ce4d518740a8470cc3a4e06.svg)\n\n- First, notice that ![](images/71/715228dc0a53bd16e4f6b055c9a4bc4679d8761e7a461e61f58003ffa8563e42.svg) are the equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/41/4186349824bab0f592f8e951f2b0ae7472e7879c693b0271af9e824bbc67f112.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/47/4734b2299d6f70fec85b91c2d6e74332ed722dc7522d497ef8bb4e80682d3052.svg) has the equation \n$y= -x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/4f/4f83cfd4f18ee670366bc8987a4c87b900ab9f5226fec8d18440627a0db6c6d0.svg)\nlie on that line. Since $e^{3t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n![](images/95/95c8829e56ce60e3fd877757c0063878e283f65a2da327bbd64ae80d725fbd81.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7b/7bb6a132d08721ef0d96e6e47dc3089de551e9551524f47de0af495a0b9418f5.png)" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186222488 1064 "Suppose a $2 \\times 2$ system has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2\\neq 0$ with eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2.$\n\nThen, the eigenvector direction for $\\lambda_1=0$ gives a **line of equilibria**:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_E = c_1 \\mathbf{v}_1, \\qquad c_1 \\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nThe other eigenvalue $\\lambda_2$ determines what happens off the equilibrium line:\n\n- If $\\lambda_2>0,$ trajectories move *away* from the equilibrium line (*unstable* direction).\n\n- If $\\lambda_2<0,$ trajectories move *toward* the equilibrium line (*stable* direction).\n\nA typical solution has the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1\\mathbf{v}_1 + c_2 e^{\\lambda_2 t}\\mathbf{v}_2,\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nSo trajectories are *parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_2,$* shifted by the equilibrium vector $c_1\\mathbf{v}_1.$\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following example\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we know the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2=3,$ with corresponding eigenvectors ![](images/47/47c0d53d1eed7289cee7d61cfaf1e8a62bccc084f445b5ca217c9b3bec78b0d8.svg) and ![](images/99/998a77eb9b491e2d1e733a0dfe1a65cc1d39759650e36c671ca1c2aa1064e29a.svg) respectively. \n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system. \n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](images/e7/e71f95804ab65c6d60a907f33967a744b13e2c977ce4d518740a8470cc3a4e06.svg)\n\n- First, notice that ![](images/71/715228dc0a53bd16e4f6b055c9a4bc4679d8761e7a461e61f58003ffa8563e42.svg) are the equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/41/4186349824bab0f592f8e951f2b0ae7472e7879c693b0271af9e824bbc67f112.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/47/4734b2299d6f70fec85b91c2d6e74332ed722dc7522d497ef8bb4e80682d3052.svg) has the equation \n$y= -x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/4f/4f83cfd4f18ee670366bc8987a4c87b900ab9f5226fec8d18440627a0db6c6d0.svg)\nlie on that line. Since $e^{3t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n![](images/95/95c8829e56ce60e3fd877757c0063878e283f65a2da327bbd64ae80d725fbd81.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7b/7bb6a132d08721ef0d96e6e47dc3089de551e9551524f47de0af495a0b9418f5.png)" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186229195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547055 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186231408 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547079 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186231409 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547032 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186278400 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y,\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6200343142274ee2f5b3934cbe014ee24edf8177f3a544c38dc070bb7df83ef.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y)=2\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{cos}^{2}⁡x+8}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{9(1+{cos}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+{cos}^{2}⁡x,$ we get $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ u & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\sqrt{u}(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2({2}^{3/2}-1) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186278400 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y,\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6200343142274ee2f5b3934cbe014ee24edf8177f3a544c38dc070bb7df83ef.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y)=2\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{cos}^{2}⁡x+8}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{9(1+{cos}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+{cos}^{2}⁡x,$ we get $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ u & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\sqrt{u}(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2({2}^{3/2}-1) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186278435 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the part of the surface $x={y}^{3}+{e}^{z}$ that lies inside the elliptical cylinder $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=1.$ From left to right, what are the missing expressions?" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186278435 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(y,z)\\,:\\,\\boxed{}\\,}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the part of the surface $x={y}^{3}+{e}^{z}$ that lies inside the elliptical cylinder $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=1.$ From left to right, what are the missing expressions?" 13194139535947 17592186045417 false] [17592186296844 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546569 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [175